    {
      "id": "48f39cbe-3b9d-4138-b96c-019e1e4543ef",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 1,
      "content": "Articulation Agreement Overview This agreement outlines lower-division course requirements specific to the major. Students are strongly encouraged to consult an academic advisor to ensure they meet all degree requirements for their intended baccalaureate program. Helpful Resources: CSUB Catalog Transfer Admission Requirements Academic Advising Student Centers CSUB Program Pathways Mapper Course Equivalency Information for 4-Year Institutions Students and staff can explore course transfer equivalencies through the Transfer Evaluation System (TES) . Equivalency agreements are updated regularly and subject to change. For the most current information, please refer to TES or contact the Articulation Office .",
      "contentHtml": "<p><strong>Articulation Agreement Overview</strong></p><p>This agreement outlines lower-division course requirements specific to the major. Students are strongly encouraged to consult an academic advisor to ensure they meet all degree requirements for their intended baccalaureate program.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>Helpful Resources:</strong></p><ul><li><a href=\"https://catalog.csub.edu/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CSUB Catalog</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/admissions/transfer\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Transfer Admission Requirements</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/advising\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Academic Advising Student Centers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/ppm\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">CSUB Program Pathways Mapper</a></li></ul><p><br></p><p><strong>Course Equivalency Information for 4-Year Institutions</strong></p><p>Students and staff can explore course transfer equivalencies through the <a href=\"https://tes.collegesource.com/publicview/TES_publicview01.aspx?rid=7ea49506-39bf-4815-8199-734369e7a30c&amp;aid=ccc830a2-2435-4dd6-b352-a586b4575654\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Transfer Evaluation System (TES)</a>. Equivalency agreements are updated regularly and subject to change. For the most current information, please refer to TES or contact the <a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/articulation/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Articulation Office</a>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "id": "f0d510d6-6383-418d-b79c-019e1e4543ef",
      "type": "GeneralTitle",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 2,
      "content": "About the Major",
      "contentHtml": null
    },
    {
      "id": "7a1b1bd5-40d1-4825-81fc-019e1e4543ef",
      "type": "GeneralText",
      "area": "General",
      "position": 3,
      "content": "The Bachelor of Arts in Sociology emphasizes the systematic study of human behavior in social groups. It focuses on how people coordinate their activities to achieve both individual and collective goals. Sociology is different from the other social and behavioral sciences in two important ways. First, it places primary importance on the social group within the larger society. Second, it seeks to explain the broad range of human behavior as it is influenced by the social context. Thus, sociology studies the major issues of our times and seeks to understand their complexity and predict their future. It investigates how patterns of social activity originate, what sustains them, why they take one form rather than another, and how they change. Depending on the particular interest of the student, an appropriate minor area of study is selected in consultation with the student’s advisor. Instead of the traditional minor, the Department also offers a concentration in either Racial and Ethnic Dynamics or Human Services. Career opportunities for sociology majors include a variety of agencies at the federal, state, and municipal levels, as well as with both private non-profit and private for-profit organizations. The Sociology program faculty stresses a close working relationship with students and strongly encourages students to take full advantage of the many opportunities the Department provides including but not limited to collaborative research with faculty, student internships, teaching assistantships, and other professional opportunities. The department’s program learning goals and objectives can be found on the department’s website. For additional information, visit the following: Department of Sociology Program Requirements - Sociology, BA",
      "contentHtml": "<p><span style=\"font-size: 12px;\">The Bachelor of Arts in Sociology emphasizes the systematic study of human behavior in social groups.&nbsp;It focuses on how people coordinate their activities to achieve both individual and collective goals.&nbsp;Sociology is different from the other social and behavioral sciences in two important ways.&nbsp;First, it places primary importance on the social group within the larger society.&nbsp;Second, it seeks to explain the broad range of human behavior as it is influenced by the social context.&nbsp;Thus, sociology studies the major issues of our times and seeks to understand their complexity and predict their future.&nbsp;It investigates how patterns of social activity originate, what sustains them, why they take one form rather than another, and how they change. Depending on the particular interest of the student, an appropriate minor area of study is selected in consultation with the student’s advisor.&nbsp;Instead of the traditional minor, the Department also offers a concentration in either Racial and Ethnic Dynamics or Human Services. Career opportunities for sociology majors include a variety of agencies at the federal, state, and municipal levels, as well as with both private non-profit and private for-profit organizations. The Sociology program faculty stresses a close working relationship with students and strongly encourages students to take full advantage of the many opportunities the Department provides including but not limited to collaborative research with faculty, student internships, teaching assistantships, and other professional opportunities. The department’s program learning goals and objectives can be found on the department’s website.</span></p><p><br></p><p>For additional information, visit the following:</p><p>Department of <a href=\"https://www.csub.edu/Sociology/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Sociology</a></p><p>Program Requirements - <a href=\"https://catalog.csub.edu/academic-degrees-programs/undergraduate-majors/sociology-ba/\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" target=\"_blank\">Sociology, BA</a></p>"
    }
  ],
